 ## Router skills
 
 When user-invoked skills multiply past what you can remember, that piled-up cognitive load is cured by a **router skill**: one user-invoked skill that names the others and when to reach for each, so the human has one skill to remember instead of many. It can only hint, never fire them: user-invoked skills have no description, so nothing but the human can reach them.
+
+## Kimi Code mechanics
+
+- Directory-form skills require both `name` and `description` in frontmatter. Keep `description` present even for user-invoked skills; Kimi uses it for the human-facing catalog but blocks automatic model invocation when requested.
+- Use `disableModelInvocation: true` for user-invoked skills. The upstream `disable-model-invocation` spelling is also accepted.
+- Use `arguments: [topic]` or another short list of named parameters instead of `argument-hint`. `$ARGUMENTS`, `$0`, `$1`, and `$<name>` are available; when the body has no argument placeholders, Kimi appends the raw arguments automatically.
+- Use `${KIMI_SKILL_DIR}` for the current skill directory. Invoke skills with `/skill:<name>`; `/<name>` is a shorthand when it does not conflict with another command.
+- `agents/openai.yaml` and Claude-specific `argument-hint` metadata are not Kimi configuration. Do not rely on them to control Kimi invocation.
+- Use the native `Skill` tool when one model-invocable inline skill calls another. Kimi rejects skills with `disableModelInvocation: true` through this tool; those remain available to the human through `/skill:<name>`.
+- For subagents, follow [the Kimi subagent adapter](../../../kimi-code/adapters/subagents.md). For independent branches use [the worktree adapter](../../../kimi-code/adapters/worktrees.md), and for interviews or approval gates use [the interaction adapter](../../../kimi-code/adapters/interaction.md).
